00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
my name is elizabeth i'm a medical  student and an artist living in london  and in this video i wanted to share my  top tips for lecture note-taking in  university chances are we won't attend  every single lecture in university  but when we do these are the things that  i've found to be helpful for me the  first thing and this is kind of a  mind shift change was realizing the  difference between  a lecture and wikipedia with ic lectures  now is that they don't  only have value in the content that is  delivered and the information  but there's a lot of value in the  lecture  as an individual in himself this is  because chances are they are a  professional  someone who has a lot of insight and  experience in what they are talking  about  so the way that they phrase things the  connections that they make the things  that they emphasize  are actually the most valuable thing for  me in the lecture what i mean by this  specifically  is that if i see a lecturer focusing on  something for quite a long time  or explicitly saying that this is very  important  this comes up all the time i really want  to focus on whenever they say things  like this  i will make a symbol for me that's a  star on the top right hand corner of my  lecture notes but you can really use  anything  so i'll just make a symbol to tell  myself later that this  is something important to know some  lecturers might have 70 or 80 slides and  it is completely impossible  for me to retain all the information  that i will be delivered  year on year in medicine but whenever  someone who's been  teaching this for many many times  explicitly says this is important  my ears per cup and i'd make a symbol in  my notes another thing that i find  important in what a lecturer says  is when they summarize things and  summaries don't only happen in the end  of the lecture although that is very  very important too  but after they finish describing a  concept especially something that is  quite long and complicated  they usually give a one sentence brief  summary  these are the most important parts of  the lecture to me i will usually write  or type these out every time  and pay a lot of attention to them in  the end of the day i can just collect  these summaries and i'll have the main  points of what i need to know in the  lecture what they might also add are  real life examples  or connections that are not mentioned in  the slide so when they say things like  the thing that i mentioned a few weeks  ago  or this is similar to the condition that  we had before  or when they describe a medication for  example they might say  oh we've seen this used previously for  why  but now we're using it for zed i know  these little connections because they're  very very important  in consolidating information in my mind  my next point would be to find the sweet  spot  there's this point in between too much  and there's too little note-taking that  depends on the individual  but it's valuable to know for me for  example if i take too many notes  it basically becomes a transcription of  what the lecture is saying and i'll end  up with four pages of lecture notes that  i'm probably never going to read again  and were a complete waste of time on the  other hand if i'm not taking any notes  at all  i am very likely to fall asleep so  there's this point in the middle that is  quite valuable  using things such as handwriting  although i do have my forms for this  too but i would definitely recommend  trying to train yourself to find what is  the amount that you  realistically would actually read when  you are revising my third point is to  only take  notes of things that are not in the  slides for things that are there already  circle it or highlight it or start it or  explain it if you don't understand and  my last point of this is so so important  is to actually ask questions to your  lecturer once it is over in my first  year i realized later on that i was  known as that really weird girl  who would ask lecturers questions after  every single one  and i genuinely would do this i'm not  shy when it comes to asking questions  and looking stupid  it's absolutely fine i do it  unintentionally enough that doing it  intentionally too sometimes  is okay so i would do after every  lecture is get yourself some questions  to ask the person  who just explained things to you there  are multiple benefits to this  firstly some things especially in  medicine would take honestly half an  hour of  googling and going down studies to find  a non-straightforward answer to  something  when i could just ask my lecturer and  get an explanation in a few seconds  because they are always on top of that  research anyway the second thing  is that they likely already understand  what they're explaining  very very well so they can give very  basic explanations  they might get carried away in a lecture  and make things a bit too complicated  but if you ask them seriously i don't  understand this  are you saying this leads to this leads  to that or am i being stupid and then i  was like oh no no no what i meant was  and they will explain it very very  simply and suddenly things will just  click in place and you have your answer  you can also ask them how important  things are  how often do we see this condition in  patients if they were to pick only one  which one would they study  what tips do they have for memorizing  these things if they've learned what  they're explaining to you  they've struggled through understanding  it too so they already have tips that  are there to give in this way in the end  of almost every lecture  i left knowing exactly what was going on  i still had a lot of memorizing  and understanding to do but especially  when things go completely over my head  in a lecture  i will make sure to approach that  lecture and ask them to clarify at least  some of it so i know where to start so  those are my top tips selection note  taking it might be that you are using  all of these methods already i'm also  curious to know what methods and tricks  you use for lecture note-taking they  would definitely help  me a lot and if there's anyone else  watching this perhaps them too  i'm thinking of potentially making a  series on how i take notes with my ipad  on  motion and with different things so if  this is something that you're interested  in too  please let me know as always if you made  it so far i hope you have a wonderful  rest of your day  be kind to yourself and others and do  believe everything you think thanks bye

